# Characterization of a Plastid Genome  
## Vitis vinifera (NC_007957.1)

Student Name: JASMIN GRACE T. BULLANDAY  
Date: October 1, 2026

## 1. Basic Information

Full scientific name: Vitis vinifera
Family: Vitaceae
NCBI accession/version: NC_007957.1
Database source: NCBI RefSeq
Complete plastid-genome size: 160,928 bp

## 2. Evidence of Completeness

The NCBI record is labeled “chloroplast, complete genome”.
It is a circular molecule of 160,928 bp.
It contains the typical quadripartite structure (LSC–IR–SSC–IR).
It has a full set of plastid genes (photosystem, ATP synthase, rbcL, rRNA, tRNA, etc.).
It is not a short barcode fragment (rbcL or matK only) or nuclear sequence.

## 3. Genome Organization
The Vitis vinifera plastid genome has the classic LSC–IRb–SSC–IRa arrangement.
Region sizes:

LSC = 89,147 bp
SSC = 19,065 bp
Each IR = 26,358 bp
Total = 160,928 bp

## 4. Gene Content
Gene content summary:

Total genes (including duplicates in IR): 131
Unique genes: 113
Protein-coding genes: 85
tRNA genes: 37
rRNA genes: 8
Pseudogenes: none major reported

Genes in the inverted-repeat (IR) regions appear in two copies because the two IR regions are identical inverted copies of each other.

## 5. Selected Protein-Coding Genes
Gene         Function
psaA       Photosystem I reaction center protein
psbA       Photosystem II reaction center protein (D1)
atpA       ATP synthase alpha subunit
petA       Cytochrome f (cytochrome b6f complex)
rbcL       Large subunit of RuBisCO (carbon fixation)
rpoB       RNA polymerase beta subunit
rpl2       Ribosomal protein L2 (large subunit)
matK       Maturase K (involved in RNA splicing)

## 6. RNA and RNA-Processing Features
Important RNA and RNA-processing features:

rRNA genes: rrn16, rrn23, rrn4.5, rrn5 (all duplicated in the IR)
tRNA gene examples: trnH-GUG, trnK-UUU, trnL-UAA, trnA-UGC
Genes with introns: trnK-UUU (contains matK), rpl2, ndhB, clpP (two introns)

## 7. Unusual Features
Unusual features:

No major gene losses, large rearrangements, or unusual pseudogenes are reported for the reference genome NC_007957.1. The gene order is highly conserved and identical to the typical tobacco-type plastid genome.

## 8. GC Content and Other Observations
GC content: 37.40 %
Two other notable observations:

The genome is a single circular molecule (confirmed by Galaxy statistics: only 1 sequence record).
The inverted-repeat regions have higher GC content (~43 %) than the LSC and SSC regions.

## 9. Plastid vs Mitochondrial Genomes
Similarities between plastid and mitochondrial genomes (at least 5):

1. Both are located in cytoplasmic organelles.
2. Both are usually circular.
3. Both contain their own DNA and some genes for their function.
4. Both are typically inherited from the maternal parent in most plants.
5. Both have multiple copies per cell.
6. Both evolve at a slower rate than the nuclear genome (though rates differ).

Differences (at least 5):

1. Location: Plastid → chloroplast; Mitochondrion → mitochondrion.
2. Main function: Plastid → photosynthesis; Mitochondrion → cellular respiration / ATP production.
3. Genome size: Plastid (~120–160 kb) is usually larger and more stable than plant mitochondrial genomes (which vary greatly).
4. Gene content: Plastids have many photosynthesis-related genes; mitochondria have fewer genes, mostly related to respiration.
5. Structural stability: Plastid genomes are highly conserved in structure; plant mitochondrial genomes frequently rearrange.
6. Mutation rate: Plastid genomes generally have lower substitution rates than plant mitochondrial genomes in many lineages.

## 10. Practical Value and Limitations
Advantages of plastid genomes compared with the nuclear genome:

- Much smaller and easier to sequence completely
- High copy number per cell (easier to amplify)
- Conserved gene content and structure across plants
- Maternal inheritance (useful for tracking maternal lineages)
- recombination rate
- Useful for phylogenetics and barcoding
- No sex chromosomes (unlike nuclear genome)

Limitations:

- Limited number of genes (does not represent the whole organism)
- Maternal inheritance only (cannot track paternal contribution)
- Not useful for studying traits controlled by nuclear genes
- Can have limited resolution for very recent divergences

Research questions:

- Plastid data useful for: “What is the phylogenetic relationship among different Vitis species or grape cultivars?”
- Nuclear data more appropriate for: “Which genes control berry size or disease resistance in grapevine?”

